On Tue, 24 Feb 2004 01:52:01 -0600, Nicholas Aodhagan Webb wrote:

> This sounds good.  I know NHINRL, but the canned meat to canned vegies
> ratio is a bit high.  Of course of the canned vegetables in real life,
> beans are the least likely to be vegan.

Well, I didn't want to deal with the idea of baked beans with pork or
bacon....
 
> You don't have to use tintxts for the names. There's got to be 15 types
> of canned beans you can use: soy(nutritious), red, black, green,
> refried, pinto, garbonzo, baked, navy, mung, lima, fava, kidney,
> cannellini, etc.

Good list; I idn't think of mung, red, or soy(!), but I did have wax.

For simplicity's sake I thought originally of just having the normal beans
be kidney(40), black(80), refried(120), and baked(200), but after glancing
at the source I thought it easier to just use the tintxts for consistency
and ease of coding. It also provides the possibility of rotten without the
extra "Blazing Saddles" effect.

>> Blessed (5%):
>> + Confer 500 Nutrition
>> 50% chance of {
>>   + Message "Holy frijoles, these must be jumping beans!" + 3 in 10
>>   chance of granting teleportitis + if teleport not granted, 1 in 12
>>   chance of teleport control + if neither intrinsic granted, Message
>>   "They don't seem hard to catch." + Message "You consume refried
>>   beans."
>> } else
>> + Message "You consume baked beans."
> 
> Still too hign.  You don't need wishing to make a tin blessed. For
> corpse tins, the chance of a teleportitis tin is in the ballpark of 1%.
> Teleport control is way too high for this.  It should be 0%. There are
> only two ways to get perm TC.  Eating tengu and eating =oTC. So there is
> a chance for vegans to get it. They just have to jump through lots of
> hoops. Putting it in a tin (even with a chance as low as 3%) seems is
> giving it away.

Monks and wizards get TC at lv 17, though they never get the teleport
intrinsic except via rings or corpses (or poly). Master key of thievery
also gives TC.

While the chance of getting a teleportitis *tin* is presently small, the
chance of getting a teleportitis *corpse* is very high: 30% for nymphs,
50% for leprechauns, 60% for tengu, all of which are fairly common. When I
think about it though, the general annoyance factor in making one of those
corpses is pretty huge. :) Saying that, though, I see a good rationale for
reducing the chances further, say to 1 in 10 for teepitis.

As for the fallthrough to TC, I figured that tengu had a 50% chance of
granting TC if either the corpse didn't grant teepitis or the ingester
already had it (did I read that wrong?). So there's a 1 in 5 chance of
getting teleport control (50% of 40%). For the beans, though, I figured
that if the ingester was granted teepitis by the tin, even if already in
possession of (by:) it, there would be no check to grant TC.

I did want to minimize the number of changes that would be required in a
patch. The easiest was to consider the tin like a level 1 tengu corpse,
but I thought that would grant even more likelihood of TC.

As for blessing tins, that would require identifying the tin first in
order to be practical. Any argument regarding blessed effects vs uncursed
is trivial for pretty much any item; I don't think the difference is
greater than for most scrolls, and I thought of the extra effect of a
cursed tin as an appropriate counterbalance. Perhaps making sure that tins
of beans would never be generated in multiples would provide greater
obscurity.

Finally, let me defuse any questions of whininess or lack of character.
Even though my default character is a monk, I've often violated vegetarian
conduct. Nothing in the beans patch will make floating eyes any less
yummy. :) I ran with the beans idea because it sounded cute and because
tinned beans are so common IRL. They were the traditional food of cowboys,
hobos, and French soldiers, and--since I'm a starving student--rank second
only to tuna on the can shelf in my kitchen.

